## Time zone and daylight saving

- **[Source]** The time column is labelled **MEZ** (Mitteleuropäische Zeit, Central
  European Time).
- **[Undocumented]** The documentation does not say whether the clock is a fixed
  UTC+1 all year or switches to summer time, and it does not state a UTC offset.
  **We do not assume the timestamps are UTC.**
- **[Measured]** In every year 2004–2025 the last-Sunday-of-March day has all
  six `02:00–02:50` records (no skipped hour) and the last-Sunday-of-October day
  has exactly six (no repeated hour). Peak mean short-wave radiation falls in the
  `12:00` label in both Dec/Jan and Jun/Jul. Both observations are what a fixed
  year-round offset would produce, and not what a DST-switching clock would
  produce. This is supporting evidence, **not** a provider confirmation.
- **Working decision:** treat timestamps as **naive local station time on a
  continuous clock** and never convert to UTC or another zone. Hour-of-day and
  season features (if ever added) should be derived from these values as-is.

## Proposed hourly preparation (recommendation)

Source sampling is 10-minute, end-of-period averages, so an hourly value should
be an aggregation of the six records in that hour:

1. Label an hour by its end instant `H:00`.
2. Its value is the **mean of the six 10-minute temperatures stamped
   `H-50 min, H-40, H-30, H-20, H-10, H:00`**. These six averages cover exactly
   `(H-1h, H]`, which respects the end-of-period convention.
3. The hour is **valid only if all six records are present on the grid and
   finite** and none equals the `-9999` sentinel. Otherwise it is marked
   **invalid** — no interpolation, forward-fill or imputation.
4. Invalid hours are kept as explicit gaps. Windows must not contain an invalid
   hour, so none span a gap.
5. Duplicate timestamps (all identical here) collapse to one record; out-of-order
   rows are placed by timestamp. Off-grid timestamps do not satisfy the rule in
   step 3. A dedicated check should fail loudly if any duplicate has conflicting
   values, since the current files have none.

Measured result: the hourly grid from 2003-11-24 16:00 to 2026-01-01 00:00 has
**193,761** hours, of which **193,548 are valid** (99.89%) and **213 are invalid**.

## Chronological split and window counts (measured)

Method: the 70/15/15 boundaries are placed on the **calendar-hour grid** (index
`floor(0.70·N)` and `floor(0.85·N)` of N = 193,761), so partitions are
contiguous time ranges even though some hours are invalid. Windows are built
**inside each partition** with stride 1; a window is 72 consecutive valid input
hours followed by 24 consecutive valid target hours (96 hours), all inside one
partition.

| Split | Hour range (labels, inclusive) | Grid hours | Valid hours | Windows (stride 1) | Longest valid run (h) |
|-------|--------------------------------|-----------:|------------:|-------------------:|----------------------:|
| Train | 2003-11-24 16:00 – 2019-05-15 23:00 | 135,632 | 135,437 | 134,677 | 31,582 |
| Validation | 2019-05-16 00:00 – 2022-09-07 23:00 | 29,064 | 29,047 | 28,762 | 19,281 |
| Test | 2022-09-08 00:00 – 2026-01-01 00:00 | 29,065 | 29,064 | 28,874 | 23,582 |

Total windows: 192,313. All three splits are large enough for the task. These
numbers are tied to the checksum manifest above; if the files change, rerun the
script and update them. Neighbouring stride-1 windows overlap almost entirely,
so the number of statistically independent test samples is far smaller than the
window count.

## Intended use

Educational: comparing a recurrent baseline with a self-attention model for
24-hour-ahead temperature forecasting, and illustrating attention weights, in a
course project.

## Limitations and out-of-scope uses

- **Not a validated local forecasting service.** The data is one rooftop station
  in Jena, Germany. It says nothing about any campus weather and the models are
  not validated for operational decisions.
- Single variable (temperature); no weather-system inputs, so long-horizon skill
  will be limited. Forecast errors can be large in rapidly changing weather.
- Station and sensor changes, siting, and long-term climate trends over 22
  years mean the later test period may differ from the training period.
- Quality control by the provider is described only as "checked for plausibility"
  **[Source]**; we did not independently validate sensor accuracy.
- Timestamp zone/DST semantics are inferred, not documented (above).
- Attribution is required under CC BY 4.0: *Max Planck Institute for
  Biogeochemistry, Jena — Weather Station Beutenberg (`mpi_roof`),
  <https://www.bgc-jena.mpg.de/wetter/>*.

## Privacy and persistence

The data is environmental measurements and contains no personal data.
User-submitted forecast inputs are a separate matter; see the project's privacy
statement (not yet written).

